  "description": {
    "ja": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 日本語版「ツルナ」",
      "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%83%84%E3%83%AB%E3%83%8A",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 109581738,
      "text": "ツルナ はハマミズナ科ツルナ属のつる性多年草。別名ハマヂシャ。多肉で海岸の砂地に生え、若い茎を摘んで食用にもされる。"
    },
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Tetragonia tetragonioides」",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tetragonia_tetragonioides",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 1375069176,
      "text": "Tetragonia tetragonioides, commonly called New Zealand spinach, warrigal greens or by its Māori name of Kōkihi, is a flowering plant in the fig-marigold family (Aizoaceae). It is often cultivated as a leafy vegetable."
    }
  },
  "sections": {
    "cultivation": {
      "ja": {
        "source": "Wikipedia 日本語版「ツルナ」の節「栽培」",
        "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%83%84%E3%83%AB%E3%83%8A#%E6%A0%BD%E5%9F%B9",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
        "revision": 109581738,
        "heading": "栽培",
        "text": "葉を食用とするために栽培することがある。栽培は容易で、海岸などで野生しているものから種子を採取し、庭やプランターに蒔いて水やりすると、10日ほどで発芽する。基肥は堆肥、腐葉土などを1 - 2握り程度与え、土に混ぜる程度でよいとされる。\n野生化した地方もあり、自生とされるものも一部は栽培から野生化した可能性がある。現代では食用としての栽培ではなく、グラウンドカバーとしても栽培される。",
        "truncated": false
      },
      "en": {
        "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Tetragonia tetragonioides」の節「Cultivation」",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tetragonia_tetragonioides#Cultivation",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
        "revision": 1375069176,
        "heading": "Cultivation",
        "text": "It is grown for the edible leaves, and can be used as food or an ornamental plant for ground cover. It can be an annual or perennial. As some of its names signify, it has similar flavour and texture properties to spinach, and is cooked like spinach. Like spinach, it contains oxalates; its medium to low levels of oxalates need to be removed by blanching the leaves in hot water for one minute, then rinsing in cold water before cooking. It thrives in hot weather, and is considered an heirloom vegetable. Few insects consume it, and even slugs and snails do not seem to feed on it.\nThe thick, irregularly-shaped seeds should be planted just after the last spring frost. Before planting, the seeds should be soaked for 12 hours in cold water, or 3 hours in warm water. Seeds should be planted 5–10 mm (0.2–0.4 in) deep, and spaced 15–30 cm (5.9–11.8 in) apart. The seedlings will emerge in 10–20 days, and it will continue to produce greens through the summer. Mature plant will self-seed. Seeds will overwinter up to USDA zone 5.",
        "truncated": false
      }
    }
  },
